10
00:01:01,515 --> 00:01:02,215
[SPEAKER_03]: The sensuality.

11
00:01:02,935 --> 00:01:04,175
[SPEAKER_04]: The principle of sensuality.

12
00:01:04,616 --> 00:01:04,916
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

13
00:01:05,596 --> 00:01:12,858
[SPEAKER_04]: What would the sense organs without the principle of sensuality, without having the principle of sensuality in them?

14
00:01:13,358 --> 00:01:15,119
[SPEAKER_04]: It would be just the senses.

15
00:01:18,104 --> 00:01:33,020
[SPEAKER_04]: And that, again, I think, probably would want to be qualified for people as meaning not just... because you have, like, when you say the senses, for example, one would think you have, like, an image of an eye, for example, or an ear.

16
00:01:34,221 --> 00:01:39,707
[SPEAKER_04]: But it really means more, like, the the senses are there where you are seeing, where you are

17
00:01:40,470 --> 00:01:54,300
[SPEAKER_04]: The kind of range of what you can experience through your senses, is what the senses are, in my understanding.

18
00:01:55,520 --> 00:02:00,184
[SPEAKER_03]: So, but can you... would you be able to experience anything through your senses?

19
00:02:01,232 --> 00:02:13,384
[SPEAKER_03]: If the senses were not there in the world as in these flesh organs existing... No, but when...

20
00:02:13,644 --> 00:02:17,608
[SPEAKER_03]: So then the question is, can you... but can you think them?

21
00:02:19,129 --> 00:02:21,411
[SPEAKER_03]: How do you know you have eyes as

22
00:02:22,728 --> 00:02:28,632
[SPEAKER_03]: As something that's necessary for you to see, even if it's like you're looking at your own eyes in the mirror.

23
00:02:28,812 --> 00:02:28,972
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

24
00:02:29,352 --> 00:02:32,054
[SPEAKER_03]: What you see in the mirror is the result of having your eyes.

25
00:02:32,234 --> 00:02:33,395
[SPEAKER_03]: It's not the eyes.

26
00:02:33,475 --> 00:02:33,695
[SPEAKER_04]: Yes.

27
00:02:34,896 --> 00:02:38,659
[SPEAKER_03]: So, what is then the eye organ in this case?

28
00:02:40,240 --> 00:02:50,887
[SPEAKER_04]: It is, well, the necessary condition for any sight, really, that what in in what whatever there is that is seen,

29
00:02:52,803 --> 00:03:03,649
[SPEAKER_04]: There is... what is, like, in common to all of that, or behind any of that, is what the eye is.

30
00:03:04,109 --> 00:03:04,849
[SPEAKER_04]: But it's...

31
00:03:05,009 --> 00:03:08,871
[SPEAKER_03]: But, practically, from your point of view, where is that eye?

32
00:03:08,971 --> 00:03:13,894
[SPEAKER_03]: Because, obviously, now you're describing it as the necessary condition, and that's all correct, theoretically.

33
00:03:14,414 --> 00:03:21,258
[SPEAKER_03]: But, practically, the eye that sees, while you're describing to me the eye that sees, where is it?

34
00:03:21,338 --> 00:03:21,718
[SPEAKER_03]: What is it?

35
00:03:23,246 --> 00:03:23,926
[SPEAKER_03]: Can you think it?

36
00:03:25,808 --> 00:03:27,729
[SPEAKER_03]: Can you... How can you relate it?

37
00:03:27,849 --> 00:03:28,950
[SPEAKER_03]: So, you will think it.

38
00:03:29,190 --> 00:03:29,410
[SPEAKER_03]: Yes.

39
00:03:29,430 --> 00:03:30,651
[SPEAKER_03]: It's not can you, can I.

40
00:03:30,691 --> 00:03:31,351
[SPEAKER_03]: You will think it.

41
00:03:31,991 --> 00:03:37,495
[SPEAKER_03]: But will the thought of your eye organ... Can it stand for the eye organ?

42
00:03:38,055 --> 00:03:39,236
[SPEAKER_03]: Or how can it do so?

43
00:03:40,136 --> 00:03:41,157
[SPEAKER_03]: Or can it not do so?

44
00:03:42,018 --> 00:03:44,799
[SPEAKER_03]: So, how can you then know it, if you can't even think it?

45
00:03:47,001 --> 00:03:48,802
[SPEAKER_04]: Well, you can kind of know it by...

46
00:03:50,364 --> 00:04:10,295
[SPEAKER_04]: By realizing that you can't think it, in a way, in the sense that you can... if you are able to distinguish the thought of the I, as in saying, okay, that's a thought that I have, and even that's on the basis of already that there are sights, that there is...

47
00:04:12,797 --> 00:04:24,501
[SPEAKER_04]: That I, I'm, that there's consciousness of sight, as in the things are there, whatever is there, that is seen, it's through, through the eye.

48
00:04:24,902 --> 00:04:28,283
[SPEAKER_04]: But, like, what's funny is that you do

49
00:04:32,481 --> 00:04:52,637
[SPEAKER_04]: You know, if I'm walking in the forest and there's like a branch that is, you know, coming across my path or something, immediately, every... you know, you know that if this branch is going to cut my eye or something, I won't... like, you know that it's a danger to you, you know that it's a problem, you know that you can't see without...

50
00:04:52,657 --> 00:04:59,062
[SPEAKER_03]: So, then, your thought of... about your eyes stands for your eyes.

51
00:04:59,082 --> 00:05:00,923
[SPEAKER_03]: Because why would you then cover your eye organ?

52
00:05:02,677 --> 00:05:05,518
[SPEAKER_03]: As a result of the thought of your eyes might be hurt.

53
00:05:06,719 --> 00:05:07,739
[SPEAKER_03]: So, it does stand for it.

54
00:05:08,340 --> 00:05:08,980
[SPEAKER_03]: So, you can't...

55
00:05:09,000 --> 00:05:10,160
[SPEAKER_03]: It does stand for it in a way, yeah.

56
00:05:10,180 --> 00:05:10,340
[SPEAKER_03]: Okay.

57
00:05:10,380 --> 00:05:12,161
[SPEAKER_03]: So, it stands in a way.

58
00:05:13,622 --> 00:05:14,742
[SPEAKER_03]: You can't...

59
00:05:15,543 --> 00:05:17,023
[SPEAKER_03]: So, what's the problem then, I suppose?

60
00:05:17,344 --> 00:05:17,964
[SPEAKER_02]: That's the question.

61
00:05:18,644 --> 00:05:22,486
[SPEAKER_03]: Where is the... Where is the mistake for somebody who is still not free from sensuality?

62
00:05:23,366 --> 00:05:29,169
[SPEAKER_03]: So, you can't but not have thoughts if you think about your organs, because that's the only way to know them.

63
00:05:30,229 --> 00:05:31,950
[SPEAKER_03]: So, where is the issue then?

64
00:05:32,492 --> 00:05:39,473
[SPEAKER_03]: Because kutujana, asuta kutujana, not free from sensuality, has thoughts about his organs and views about his organs.

65
00:05:40,274 --> 00:05:44,995
[SPEAKER_03]: And now, seemingly, even the one who is free from sensuality would still have thoughts about the organs.

66
00:05:45,035 --> 00:05:45,235
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

67
00:05:45,715 --> 00:05:47,115
[SPEAKER_03]: What's the difference in those thoughts?

68
00:05:47,315 --> 00:05:48,095
[SPEAKER_03]: Where is the difference?

69
00:05:48,795 --> 00:05:49,115
[SPEAKER_03]: Anyone?

70
00:05:50,496 --> 00:05:59,078
[SPEAKER_03]: The assumption... the assuming... How does that manifest as a difference?

71
00:05:59,678 --> 00:06:01,198
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, with sensuality... Are you assuming what?

72
00:06:02,612 --> 00:06:07,273
[SPEAKER_03]: So, the first person has thoughts about the organs, they're not free from sensuality.

73
00:06:07,333 --> 00:06:12,774
[SPEAKER_03]: Another person has the thought about their sense organs, they're free from sensuality.

74
00:06:12,914 --> 00:06:13,994
[SPEAKER_03]: What is the assumption there?

75
00:06:16,275 --> 00:06:23,636
[SPEAKER_00]: That that thought represents an external... yeah, something external to it.

76
00:06:23,656 --> 00:06:24,396
[SPEAKER_03]: Okay, yeah.

77
00:06:24,876 --> 00:06:28,157
[SPEAKER_03]: So, that kind of comes down to...

78
00:06:29,299 --> 00:06:30,880
[SPEAKER_03]: What you put first, that's it.

79
00:06:32,220 --> 00:06:39,683
[SPEAKER_03]: So, both times you have a thought in regard to the sense organs, but the first time you put your thought and your sense of self first.

80
00:06:42,364 --> 00:06:55,889
[SPEAKER_03]: The second instance, the one who is free from sensuality, recognizes the thought as a necessary presence, as a necessary way of a organ to be present, but it does not misconceive the organ to be in the thought.

81
00:06:56,842 --> 00:07:03,987
[SPEAKER_03]: It recognizes that inaccessible physicality, rūpa, present, there, enduring, because of which you are having a thought of it.

82
00:07:04,948 --> 00:07:12,653
[SPEAKER_03]: But it does not conceive that thought to be the first, or to be in rūpa, or rūpa in it, or apart from it, or all these permutations.

83
00:07:13,273 --> 00:07:19,818
[SPEAKER_03]: So you understand, basically, thought for what it is, and it ceases to misrepresent the order of things.

84
00:07:19,858 --> 00:07:20,218
[SPEAKER_03]: That's it.

85
00:07:21,467 --> 00:07:28,012
[SPEAKER_03]: Because if you don't cease to misrepresent the order of things, then things can become mystical, overly mystical.

86
00:07:28,212 --> 00:07:32,536
[SPEAKER_03]: Like, in a sense, all right, so, you know, there are these real senses that I cannot...

87
00:07:32,736 --> 00:07:34,317
[SPEAKER_03]: I can think of, but I cannot access.

88
00:07:34,337 --> 00:07:39,281
[SPEAKER_03]: But, see, saying the real senses creates another sense, or it's just another thought.

89
00:07:40,102 --> 00:07:42,805
[SPEAKER_03]: Another idea of reality, which is again idea, a thought.

90
00:07:42,865 --> 00:07:47,109
[SPEAKER_03]: So, the point is not to discover what's behind your thought, because it's just going to be another thought.

91
00:07:47,610 --> 00:07:51,074
[SPEAKER_03]: The point is to stop putting that thought in a wrong place.

92
00:07:51,174 --> 00:07:51,734
[SPEAKER_03]: Let's say like that.

93
00:07:51,954 --> 00:07:54,858
[SPEAKER_03]: Stop assuming it as first, in regard to what you're trying to think.

94
00:07:57,460 --> 00:08:01,504
[SPEAKER_03]: And that's... that comes down to practically that whole difference between the

95
00:08:03,222 --> 00:08:17,360
[SPEAKER_03]: I'm not sure we should really get into that now, because it might take us in a different direction, but between, like, what's peripherally present while you're thinking, which is another thought, but there is a difference in the nature of thought if it's actually thought and if it's peripherally enduring.

96
00:08:18,680 --> 00:08:24,224
[SPEAKER_03]: If you assume the external point of view, then, yes, you can say, oh, these are all thoughts, but we are not talking about external point of view.

97
00:08:24,244 --> 00:08:32,490
[SPEAKER_03]: We are talking about personal, practical point of view, whereby you are thinking something, which means other things that are not directly thought are peripherally present.

98
00:08:32,550 --> 00:08:36,253
[SPEAKER_03]: So, seeing the difference between the two is where you would undo this wrong order.

99
00:08:38,874 --> 00:08:42,197
[SPEAKER_02]: Would that be the difference between conceiving and thinking?

100
00:08:43,456 --> 00:08:48,179
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, there will be the difference between thinking free from conceiving and thinking not free from conceiving.

101
00:08:48,559 --> 00:08:56,244
[SPEAKER_03]: In other words, thinking results in conceiving, or perpetuates conceiving, and then thinking that it's completely free from conceiving.

102
00:08:56,404 --> 00:08:58,905
[SPEAKER_03]: Either way, exactly, you cannot have thoughts.

103
00:08:59,506 --> 00:09:10,112
[SPEAKER_03]: And people who believe that you cannot have thoughts, and have sort of access to a reality beyond your thoughts, are completely unaware of the blatant contradiction that that all is another thought.

104
00:09:10,732 --> 00:09:15,181
[SPEAKER_03]: Otherwise, how can you even refer to anything outside of your thought, unless you're already thinking it?

105
00:09:15,962 --> 00:09:18,066
[SPEAKER_03]: So, the statement becomes contradiction in terms.

106
00:09:21,172 --> 00:09:22,375
[SPEAKER_04]: I have a friend who's a doctor.

107
00:09:22,989 --> 00:09:33,320
[SPEAKER_04]: And she was saying to me how she finds it strange how, as a doctor, or anybody who studies to become a doctor, like, they study the body very closely.

108
00:09:33,681 --> 00:09:39,087
[SPEAKER_04]: And she said, I know so well all, I know all the types of diseases that the body can have.

109
00:09:39,787 --> 00:09:41,389
[SPEAKER_04]: And, like, I've seen it laid out.

110
00:09:41,429 --> 00:09:42,370
[SPEAKER_04]: I've done, you know,

111
00:09:43,511 --> 00:09:46,072
[SPEAKER_04]: Dissections of dead human bodies.

112
00:09:46,693 --> 00:09:50,974
[SPEAKER_04]: So, how come I don't see the problem with having a body?

113
00:09:51,114 --> 00:09:51,995
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah.

114
00:09:52,035 --> 00:10:05,821
[SPEAKER_04]: So, I find that a good example of what you just said, like the difference between the peripheral thinking in regard to the body, as in seeing it as wherever you go, that this body is there with you.

115
00:10:05,841 --> 00:10:06,041
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

116
00:10:06,061 --> 00:10:08,302
[SPEAKER_03]: Basically, what happens is that people observe.

117
00:10:08,802 --> 00:10:10,023
[SPEAKER_03]: You can observe other bodies.

118
00:10:10,783 --> 00:10:16,386
[SPEAKER_03]: But that becomes basically the extension of your already misconceiving your own senses.

119
00:10:17,047 --> 00:10:22,149
[SPEAKER_03]: Because you forget that you cannot observe your senses in the same sense you can observe the senses of others.

120
00:10:22,710 --> 00:10:28,233
[SPEAKER_03]: Now, I can learn about my eyes by, you know, observing your eyes and your sense organs.

121
00:10:29,083 --> 00:10:32,766
[SPEAKER_03]: But that does not necessarily imply that I need to misconceive mine.

122
00:10:33,067 --> 00:10:43,396
[SPEAKER_03]: And you misconceive your organs when you put the idea, the view about it first, and then kind of assume you're that kind of external position to your own senses.

123
00:10:43,456 --> 00:10:47,279
[SPEAKER_03]: Thus, by looking at your eyes, yes, that's what my eyes are as well.

124
00:10:47,460 --> 00:10:49,742
[SPEAKER_03]: But your eyes can never be seen in that way.

125
00:10:50,707 --> 00:11:00,709
[SPEAKER_03]: So, you can contemplate things externally, as the suttas say, but not losing that peripheral perspective that your eyes can never be seen like that.

126
00:11:00,789 --> 00:11:19,793
[SPEAKER_03]: So, by seeing the external, you know, bodies dying or decomposing, or all those asubha contemplations, for example, you then infer that these enduring sense organs that are peripherally present, the basis for my observation of what I'm looking at, are subjected to the same nature, not the specifics of it.

127
00:11:20,626 --> 00:11:29,289
[SPEAKER_03]: And that's how you get to then undermine that misconceiving and ownership and everything else.

128
00:11:29,329 --> 00:11:31,169
[SPEAKER_03]: And that is the extent of sensuality.

129
00:11:31,349 --> 00:11:33,670
[SPEAKER_03]: The extent of sensuality is the perversion of the order.

130
00:11:33,990 --> 00:11:43,133
[SPEAKER_03]: You unpervert the order, there is no more pursuit of pleasures externally, because it becomes inconceivable.

131
00:11:46,161 --> 00:11:54,003
[SPEAKER_03]: But, in order to be able to unpervert the order, you have to stop maintaining its perversion through actions of body, speech and mind.

132
00:11:54,744 --> 00:11:56,384
[SPEAKER_03]: Like, a person can think all they want.

133
00:11:57,184 --> 00:12:01,306
[SPEAKER_03]: Oh, I'm not misconceiving my senses and I'm contemplating saḷāyātana and so on.

134
00:12:01,326 --> 00:12:15,150
[SPEAKER_03]: But, if body, speech and mind are not withdrawn from sensuality and pursuit of pleasure on account of sense objects, they're perpetuating the perversion of the order and the misconceiving, because the valuing the pleasures of the sense objects is the result of that perversion.

135
00:12:16,735 --> 00:12:18,676
[SPEAKER_03]: So, then, you can do all the contemplations you want.

136
00:12:19,357 --> 00:12:22,799
[SPEAKER_03]: If your actions are still perpetuating the problem, you will not uproot it.

137
00:12:30,824 --> 00:12:39,729
[SPEAKER_03]: But could you uproot the problem of sensuality simply by being restrained, celibate, keeping the precepts?

138
00:12:41,272 --> 00:12:41,632
[SPEAKER_00]: Why not?

139
00:12:41,812 --> 00:12:43,093
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it's...

140
00:12:44,193 --> 00:12:49,255
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, that's a necessary prerequisite, but it's an assumption.

141
00:12:49,275 --> 00:12:51,195
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you have to see...

142
00:12:51,215 --> 00:12:53,496
[SPEAKER_03]: So, say somebody sticks to it.

143
00:12:54,036 --> 00:13:01,519
[SPEAKER_03]: Somebody keeps the precepts, guards the sense doors.

144
00:13:01,959 --> 00:13:04,760
[SPEAKER_03]: What... just on its own, what would that result in?

145
00:13:05,080 --> 00:13:06,841
[SPEAKER_03]: What could that result in?

146
00:13:19,779 --> 00:13:21,882
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, the strengthening of the mind and

147
00:13:27,951 --> 00:13:32,454
[SPEAKER_00]: It's going in the right direction of understanding.

148
00:13:32,474 --> 00:13:34,596
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's making...

149
00:13:34,696 --> 00:13:36,817
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it would still make a difference.

150
00:13:36,897 --> 00:13:37,478
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, for sure.

151
00:13:38,339 --> 00:13:44,483
[SPEAKER_03]: You cannot necessarily... well, you should not expect just on its own to undo all the work.

152
00:13:45,124 --> 00:13:47,165
[SPEAKER_03]: But it is part of the work.

153
00:13:47,566 --> 00:13:56,753
[SPEAKER_03]: So, obviously, just to qualify, if you're keeping the precepts regarding the sense doors correctly,

154
00:13:58,123 --> 00:14:04,370
[SPEAKER_03]: As in, if you take it on as your practice, that reveals your intentions for which you feel responsible and weighed under them.

155
00:14:05,051 --> 00:14:14,902
[SPEAKER_03]: Not if you take virtue, precepts, rules, as a form of, oh, this is a tradition, this is our authority, this community here does that, so I must do that.

156
00:14:15,377 --> 00:14:16,638
[SPEAKER_00]: That's also an assumption.

157
00:14:16,738 --> 00:14:18,640
[SPEAKER_03]: Exactly.

158
00:14:18,900 --> 00:14:31,271
[SPEAKER_03]: So, in that sense, that's just going to probably make you some merit by not engaging with sensuality, but it will not bring you a step closer towards understanding even sensuality, let alone the, you know, atavāda and all the other assumptions.

159
00:14:31,311 --> 00:14:38,858
[SPEAKER_03]: It needs to be taken personally, without the external recourse of, oh, this is the environment that keeps it for me.

160
00:14:40,549 --> 00:14:44,274
[SPEAKER_03]: So, you can obviously use the environment that supports you for the precepts.

161
00:14:44,294 --> 00:14:52,044
[SPEAKER_03]: They will not bombard you with sort of distractions and challenges, but you need to be feeling it on the level of your intentions.

162
00:14:53,048 --> 00:15:13,736
[SPEAKER_03]: And then, even without any instruction, further instruction of dhamma, if you're actually doing it on that level, it is even possible to see the danger in sensuality on your own, simply by abstaining from it, but not in that blind sense, I'm holding these duties religiously because I'm a Buddhist, or because Ajahn said so, or because the community or the tradition or my upbringing.

163
00:15:13,756 --> 00:15:14,116
[SPEAKER_03]: No, no, no.

164
00:15:14,596 --> 00:15:19,038
[SPEAKER_03]: Because I value development of mind through, kind of, discernment

165
00:15:19,878 --> 00:15:22,060
[SPEAKER_03]: And training in regard to my own intentions.

166
00:15:22,320 --> 00:15:25,143
[SPEAKER_03]: Then, if you stick to it, the danger will become apparent.

167
00:15:25,283 --> 00:15:27,725
[SPEAKER_03]: That's why an outsider can become free from sensuality.

168
00:15:29,367 --> 00:15:31,208
[SPEAKER_03]: But, not again...

169
00:15:31,489 --> 00:15:38,715
[SPEAKER_03]: I keep saying that, because people often overlook this, and then they think, okay, so I'll just keep the precepts, and it's like, well, yeah, but how do you keep them?

170
00:15:39,055 --> 00:15:40,437
[SPEAKER_03]: That's a very relevant part.

171
00:15:40,877 --> 00:15:42,639
[SPEAKER_03]: That is the sīlabhātaparamāsa part.

172
00:15:42,659 --> 00:15:45,101
[SPEAKER_03]: That's like the the first three fetches, so...

173
00:15:46,488 --> 00:15:48,568
[SPEAKER_03]: So, they need to be kept rightly.

174
00:15:49,068 --> 00:15:50,969
[SPEAKER_03]: They need to be felt rightly.

175
00:15:51,349 --> 00:15:53,069
[SPEAKER_00]: Feel the weight of the responsibility.

176
00:15:53,089 --> 00:15:53,809
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

