[SPEAKER_03]: Blanket.

353
00:31:54,919 --> 00:31:55,399
[SPEAKER_03]: Blanket, yes.

354
00:31:55,739 --> 00:31:55,979
[SPEAKER_03]: Always.

355
00:31:56,019 --> 00:31:57,019
[SPEAKER_03]: You can always engage with it.

356
00:31:57,900 --> 00:32:02,781
[SPEAKER_03]: Or other certain smell, taste, touches that always imply delight.

357
00:32:03,361 --> 00:32:04,001
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, yeah.

358
00:32:04,021 --> 00:32:06,362
[SPEAKER_00]: Things like sexuality and...

359
00:32:06,782 --> 00:32:07,162
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

360
00:32:07,202 --> 00:32:08,743
[SPEAKER_03]: Things that are against the precepts.

361
00:32:08,763 --> 00:32:09,163
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

362
00:32:09,603 --> 00:32:11,444
[SPEAKER_03]: Always imply delight.

363
00:32:11,744 --> 00:32:12,004
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

364
00:32:12,224 --> 00:32:14,184
[SPEAKER_03]: Always imply perversion of that order.

365
00:32:14,345 --> 00:32:14,505
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

366
00:32:15,145 --> 00:32:18,966
[SPEAKER_03]: But yes, once you say no to those things, then

367
00:32:20,523 --> 00:32:27,528
[SPEAKER_03]: Some engagement with sense objects, whether they're agreeable, disagreeable, or pleasant or not, can sometimes be agreeable, sometimes not.

368
00:32:28,209 --> 00:32:32,432
[SPEAKER_03]: Which means you could remove the delight from those things.

369
00:32:32,552 --> 00:32:33,753
[SPEAKER_03]: So, what is the delight then?

370
00:32:34,634 --> 00:32:35,394
[SPEAKER_03]: What is the delight?

371
00:32:36,095 --> 00:32:40,938
[SPEAKER_00]: It's on the level of the possibility, in the sense that, yeah, you could experience...

372
00:32:40,958 --> 00:32:47,123
[SPEAKER_03]: But practically, practically, if I'm not thinking about possibilities, if those are things a bit too subtle for me,

373
00:32:49,380 --> 00:32:53,763
[SPEAKER_00]: Practically, where is this delight that I obviously have because I obviously pursue sensuality.

374
00:32:53,883 --> 00:32:55,004
[SPEAKER_00]: So, wanting more.

375
00:32:55,284 --> 00:32:57,725
[SPEAKER_00]: So, wanting more pleasure, wanting to get rid of the pain.

376
00:32:58,206 --> 00:32:59,587
[SPEAKER_00]: To get rid of the pain, ultimately.

377
00:32:59,607 --> 00:32:59,787
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

378
00:33:00,107 --> 00:33:06,831
[SPEAKER_03]: So, then, the the wanting of it... Again, you know, I might not think in terms of getting rid of the pain.

379
00:33:07,452 --> 00:33:08,592
[SPEAKER_03]: You just feel the pressure.

380
00:33:09,173 --> 00:33:11,274
[SPEAKER_03]: The pull.

381
00:33:11,894 --> 00:33:14,176
[SPEAKER_03]: The intensity of sensual desire.

382
00:33:14,416 --> 00:33:14,516
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

383
00:33:14,796 --> 00:33:15,937
[SPEAKER_03]: And then you give in to it.

384
00:33:17,918 --> 00:33:18,699
[SPEAKER_03]: Where is the delight?

385
00:33:20,021 --> 00:33:22,383
[SPEAKER_00]: The possibility of getting rid of that pain.

386
00:33:23,424 --> 00:33:24,645
[SPEAKER_03]: No, but where is my delight?

387
00:33:24,665 --> 00:33:26,426
[SPEAKER_03]: See, possibilities arise as part of things.

388
00:33:26,486 --> 00:33:27,207
[SPEAKER_03]: Delight doesn't.

389
00:33:27,707 --> 00:33:31,490
[SPEAKER_03]: If delight were truly the possibility, you wouldn't be able to free yourself from it.

390
00:33:33,131 --> 00:33:37,455
[SPEAKER_03]: So, yes, there are possibilities of getting rid of it, but I am the one delighting.

391
00:33:39,436 --> 00:33:41,578
[SPEAKER_03]: There is something you are perpetuating.

392
00:33:44,521 --> 00:33:47,483
[SPEAKER_03]: So, how do you not perpetuate your delight in sensuality anymore?

393
00:33:54,230 --> 00:33:54,790
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm not choosing.

394
00:33:56,892 --> 00:33:57,933
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm choosing not to delight.

395
00:33:59,334 --> 00:34:02,916
[SPEAKER_03]: If I'm the one delighting... Can you just choose to not delight?

396
00:34:03,177 --> 00:34:06,419
[SPEAKER_03]: Because you're already, you know, undermined by a delight.

397
00:34:07,019 --> 00:34:10,922
[SPEAKER_03]: You don't start with a neutral position where delight comes and then you choose it not.

398
00:34:10,982 --> 00:34:20,049
[SPEAKER_03]: Maybe, you know, somebody who's been practicing longer can see it coming before it comes, but somebody who's not free from sensuality are always undermined by their delight.

399
00:34:22,196 --> 00:34:29,418
[SPEAKER_03]: So, in other words, you cannot not delight by choice, immediately, certainly, but how do you start uprooting it, so it can become a choice?

400
00:34:32,619 --> 00:34:46,703
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, so, eh... allowing... yeah, I mean, obviously, virtue and self-restraint, so allowing the... this discomfort to endure...

401
00:34:47,143 --> 00:34:47,523
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

402
00:34:47,543 --> 00:34:48,783
[SPEAKER_03]: ...without acting out of it.

403
00:34:49,023 --> 00:34:50,644
[SPEAKER_03]: That will basically... See,

404
00:34:51,686 --> 00:34:58,892
[SPEAKER_03]: Delight is always, exactly as you said before, exemplified through this possibility of dealing with the discomfort.

405
00:34:59,032 --> 00:34:59,352
[SPEAKER_03]: Dealing...

406
00:34:59,693 --> 00:35:02,715
[SPEAKER_03]: So, by welcoming it, welcoming that possibility, you are delighting.

407
00:35:03,476 --> 00:35:04,657
[SPEAKER_03]: And you will do that, inevitably.

408
00:35:04,697 --> 00:35:06,138
[SPEAKER_03]: You can't just say no to that.

409
00:35:06,599 --> 00:35:09,741
[SPEAKER_03]: But what you don't have to do is follow up on that delight.

410
00:35:10,862 --> 00:35:18,508
[SPEAKER_03]: So, you welcome the possibility to get rid of the unpleasant itch and get pleasure as a result of it.

411
00:35:19,725 --> 00:35:23,948
[SPEAKER_03]: Mind is elated at the possibility of it, and you endure that now.

412
00:35:25,169 --> 00:35:36,337
[SPEAKER_03]: And that is much harder, and it becomes, obviously, much more refined, than just saying no to things right, kind of, on the base level, and trying to prevent them from even arising.

413
00:35:36,357 --> 00:35:36,697
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

414
00:35:37,698 --> 00:35:40,240
[SPEAKER_03]: Oh, this mustn't even... No, no, that's the, kind of, whole point.

415
00:35:40,360 --> 00:35:43,823
[SPEAKER_03]: Yes, you say no to base things, such as things that are against the precepts,

416
00:35:44,873 --> 00:35:47,896
[SPEAKER_03]: Things will start arising, delight will start happening.

417
00:35:47,916 --> 00:35:49,338
[SPEAKER_03]: You don't want to get rid of it.

418
00:35:49,858 --> 00:35:52,461
[SPEAKER_03]: You want to be able to endure it without acting out of it.

419
00:35:52,982 --> 00:35:57,026
[SPEAKER_03]: So, in other words, people still delight because they don't see the extent of the delight.

420
00:35:57,707 --> 00:35:58,047
[SPEAKER_03]: That's it.

421
00:35:58,608 --> 00:36:03,533
[SPEAKER_03]: By seeing the extent of the delight and enduring it sufficiently enough, you stop delighting in it.

422
00:36:04,836 --> 00:36:06,938
[SPEAKER_03]: And then, the gratification is understood.

423
00:36:07,659 --> 00:36:08,960
[SPEAKER_03]: Then, the danger is understood.

424
00:36:09,561 --> 00:36:10,883
[SPEAKER_03]: And then, the escape is understood.

425
00:36:10,923 --> 00:36:16,749
[SPEAKER_03]: Escape is not pulling your eyes out or never seeing anything agreeable that was last provoking, like before.

426
00:36:17,209 --> 00:36:22,915
[SPEAKER_03]: The danger is being unable to accept the prospect of delight, to delight in it.

427
00:36:24,477 --> 00:36:24,657
[SPEAKER_03]: Why?

428
00:36:24,677 --> 00:36:25,718
[SPEAKER_03]: Because you understood the danger.

429
00:36:27,912 --> 00:36:31,934
[SPEAKER_03]: So, the danger is of the delight, on the level of mental attitude of delight.

430
00:36:32,314 --> 00:36:33,234
[SPEAKER_03]: That's the peril.

431
00:36:33,274 --> 00:36:37,396
[SPEAKER_03]: That's the biting of the bait that then you're controlled by.

432
00:36:39,256 --> 00:36:43,558
[SPEAKER_03]: Not the actual beautiful things in the world that are agreeable and people lust for.

433
00:36:44,503 --> 00:37:04,033
[SPEAKER_03]: But, yes, in order to get there, first you need to say no to those things, to engage with those things, but then you need to take it on on that internal level, and it's much sharper there, than if our practice is to just sort of adhere and protect and control the circumstances, so that nothing even arises, nothing even crosses my field of vision.

434
00:37:04,093 --> 00:37:08,316
[SPEAKER_03]: It's like, yeah, you don't seek it out, but inevitably things will.

435
00:37:09,894 --> 00:37:11,775
[SPEAKER_03]: Provoke lust and delight in your mind.

436
00:37:12,255 --> 00:37:13,055
[SPEAKER_03]: So what do you do then?

437
00:37:13,075 --> 00:37:20,838
[SPEAKER_03]: You try to negate it, deny it, cover it up, get rid of it, or actually learn how to endure it to its full extent without acting out.

438
00:37:21,198 --> 00:37:22,639
[SPEAKER_03]: And that's why people are afraid to endure it.

439
00:37:24,059 --> 00:37:25,860
[SPEAKER_03]: Because they're doubtful whether they're liked out or not.

440
00:37:27,748 --> 00:37:32,050
[SPEAKER_03]: And you don't want to endure it, because you might act out, and then wait is even more.

441
00:37:32,810 --> 00:37:35,331
[SPEAKER_03]: I might act out, and undermine everything I work for.

442
00:37:35,411 --> 00:37:36,032
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, exactly.

443
00:37:36,112 --> 00:37:36,892
[SPEAKER_03]: That's the danger.

444
00:37:36,932 --> 00:37:37,652
[SPEAKER_03]: That's the peril.

445
00:37:38,032 --> 00:37:39,753
[SPEAKER_03]: You endure it long enough, it will become apparent.

446
00:37:40,333 --> 00:37:50,037
[SPEAKER_03]: But if you don't want to let it endure in that sense, and just double down, and redefine the rule, and introduce more rules, that this never ever happens, it's like, you're fighting the problem in a wrong place.

447
00:37:54,359 --> 00:38:05,006
[SPEAKER_00]: You're not seeing that it's arisen by itself, so you're not able to see the extent, because you're trying to put yourself... Well, yeah, you're not able to see the extent, because you're unable to endure its extent.

448
00:38:05,766 --> 00:38:15,713
[SPEAKER_03]: Like, you can see and not see and stuff, but if you're able to endure it without acting out of... it doesn't matter how refined it gets, endure it,

449
00:38:17,102 --> 00:38:18,683
[SPEAKER_03]: It will be seen, the delight.

450
00:38:19,283 --> 00:38:21,484
[SPEAKER_03]: Like, you're already delighting, as I said.

451
00:38:21,904 --> 00:38:22,905
[SPEAKER_03]: It's already welcomed.

452
00:38:23,505 --> 00:38:23,745
[SPEAKER_03]: Okay.

453
00:38:24,005 --> 00:38:24,786
[SPEAKER_03]: I already welcomed this.

454
00:38:24,806 --> 00:38:25,706
[SPEAKER_03]: How do you stop it?

455
00:38:26,126 --> 00:38:26,266
[SPEAKER_03]: No.

456
00:38:26,326 --> 00:38:29,708
[SPEAKER_03]: See, now you're trying to stop it and everything, to get rid of what you already welcomed.

457
00:38:29,748 --> 00:38:29,888
[SPEAKER_03]: No.

458
00:38:30,348 --> 00:38:34,370
[SPEAKER_03]: You need to endure what you already welcomed, to see the mistake of welcoming it.

459
00:38:35,691 --> 00:38:36,451
[SPEAKER_03]: Not to cover it up.

460
00:38:36,992 --> 00:38:37,792
[SPEAKER_03]: And that's the difference.

461
00:38:54,035 --> 00:39:14,304
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you mentioned these contemplations, for example, like a subha, and, yeah, like, one interpretation of a subha is, yeah, like, the the ugly, yeah, you contemplate the ugly, like,

462
00:39:15,810 --> 00:39:18,693
[SPEAKER_00]: As a means of replacing sensuality.

463
00:39:19,293 --> 00:39:20,334
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, that's the problem, yeah.

464
00:39:20,515 --> 00:39:22,597
[SPEAKER_00]: Rather than seeing sensuality is ugly.

465
00:39:23,077 --> 00:39:23,357
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

466
00:39:23,377 --> 00:39:33,627
[SPEAKER_03]: And that's where you... You're trying to paste over the view, the idea of ugly, ugly, ugly, ugly, ugly, to kind of push away the unpleasantness of having to endure the delighted sensuality.

467
00:39:34,568 --> 00:39:46,374
[SPEAKER_03]: Instead, the true asubha practice is on the level of if you have endured it already sufficiently enough, you get to see the ugly in it, and then the endurance becomes pleasant.

468
00:39:46,514 --> 00:39:46,714
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

469
00:39:47,534 --> 00:39:49,795
[SPEAKER_03]: Becomes equated with freedom, renunciation.

470
00:39:49,855 --> 00:39:50,856
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

471
00:39:51,056 --> 00:39:56,298
[SPEAKER_03]: But if you're doing it in response to not wanting to endure it because it's unpleasant, then it's just a cover-up.

472
00:39:56,318 --> 00:39:56,478
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

473
00:39:56,698 --> 00:39:57,759
[SPEAKER_03]: Then it's not done rightly.

474
00:39:58,798 --> 00:40:01,459
[SPEAKER_02]: Then, the wrong order still remains unchanged.

475
00:40:01,499 --> 00:40:03,661
[SPEAKER_00]: So, all of these contemplations, they require this.

476
00:40:04,221 --> 00:40:04,901
[SPEAKER_03]: Yes.

477
00:40:04,941 --> 00:40:10,304
[SPEAKER_03]: As I say often, that kind of right endurance is the basis for any dhamma approach to apply.

478
00:40:22,231 --> 00:40:24,032
[SPEAKER_00]: Same with, like, ānāpānasati.

479
00:40:24,092 --> 00:40:24,252
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

480
00:40:34,068 --> 00:40:53,013
[SPEAKER_04]: The light in the... different foods of the different senses, to put it like that, as in, like, if the senses are like these six hungry animals, like in that simile.

481
00:40:56,114 --> 00:41:00,035
[SPEAKER_04]: Then...

482
00:41:00,575 --> 00:41:02,236
[SPEAKER_04]: I find it a good image because

483
00:41:05,160 --> 00:41:20,355
[SPEAKER_04]: It really is like that, that these senses are just kind of dragging you and pulling you in the different directions, and the reason that you keep following them, or kind of running after them, that's kind of, in a way, the delight.

484
00:41:20,375 --> 00:41:23,399
[SPEAKER_04]: Like, when you delight in something, that the senses are kind of...

485
00:41:24,519 --> 00:41:29,243
[SPEAKER_04]: dragging you towards, going towards it with them, you don't feel like you're being dragged.

486
00:41:30,003 --> 00:41:42,712
[SPEAKER_04]: So, it's like that's the only feeling of kind of relief or peace, or you're kind of simulating the feeling of relief or peace by kind of following after them, so that you don't see that it's actually them dragging you, not you.

487
00:41:43,170 --> 00:41:45,511
[SPEAKER_03]: So, you don't want to endure the discomfort of being dragged.

488
00:41:45,551 --> 00:41:50,714
[SPEAKER_03]: See, if you stop resisting being dragged around, if you start running with it, the tension will disappear.

489
00:41:51,674 --> 00:41:59,218
[SPEAKER_03]: The dog is yanking you and you try to fight it and then you get all this all this tension and pain and you have to fight it for who knows how long.

490
00:42:00,180 --> 00:42:07,230
[SPEAKER_03]: But if you just start running with it, oh, you don't feel the tension anymore, but you realize, but, yeah, now I have to run, I have to keep moving.

491
00:42:07,350 --> 00:42:07,851
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, exactly.

492
00:42:07,891 --> 00:42:08,492
[SPEAKER_03]: All the time.

493
00:42:08,632 --> 00:42:09,674
[SPEAKER_03]: All the time.

494
00:42:09,734 --> 00:42:14,761
[SPEAKER_03]: And that's exactly why people would delight, because they don't want to go through discomfort of...

495
00:42:15,542 --> 00:42:18,484
[SPEAKER_03]: Already hungry, wild, untamed senses.

496
00:42:19,104 --> 00:42:21,085
[SPEAKER_03]: So, as I said before, you have an option.

497
00:42:21,125 --> 00:42:33,072
[SPEAKER_03]: You can, yes, keep enabling the wild animal, so it doesn't harass you, or you can endure the harassment, so the animal will calm down, and you can finally train it, and then you don't need to run, nor do you need to fight the attention.

498
00:42:33,866 --> 00:42:38,170
[SPEAKER_04]: But for somebody who's already... that's the thing, that's why you have to start with the... Stop.

499
00:42:38,190 --> 00:42:38,730
[SPEAKER_04]: With stopping.

500
00:42:38,770 --> 00:42:41,973
[SPEAKER_03]: Firstly, yeah, you've got to stand your ground and just hold on to that lesion.

501
00:42:42,213 --> 00:42:45,616
[SPEAKER_04]: Because otherwise you don't even realize that... That's the... that's the simile, wasn't it?

502
00:42:45,636 --> 00:42:47,317
[SPEAKER_03]: The taming of the elephant.

503
00:42:47,678 --> 00:42:52,862
[SPEAKER_03]: Like, you don't bring a wild elephant and immediately start issuing instructions to it, expecting it to obey.

504
00:42:53,022 --> 00:42:53,182
[SPEAKER_03]: No.

505
00:42:53,363 --> 00:43:00,869
[SPEAKER_03]: And then you strap it down to a strong post, tie it down, and it will be kicking, screaming, urinating, defecating, going mental.

506
00:43:01,650 --> 00:43:01,890
[SPEAKER_03]: But...

507
00:43:02,560 --> 00:43:04,702
[SPEAKER_03]: It will have to come down, because it will tire itself out.

508
00:43:06,223 --> 00:43:11,426
[SPEAKER_03]: And if you don't let it run away, if you don't sort of unhook it, it will come down.

509
00:43:11,666 --> 00:43:15,389
[SPEAKER_03]: And then, you know, it will start taking food, and then it will start listening to you.

510
00:43:15,989 --> 00:43:18,011
[SPEAKER_03]: And so you broke it down, broke it in.

511
00:43:18,631 --> 00:43:20,593
[SPEAKER_03]: And that's, that's exactly the training.

512
00:43:21,333 --> 00:43:25,876
[SPEAKER_03]: Like, you can do it, you know, some people can do it more gradually, some...

513
00:43:25,896 --> 00:43:30,700
[SPEAKER_03]: But the point is, yeah, you gotta start containing yourself from spilling outwards,

514
00:43:31,735 --> 00:43:34,536
[SPEAKER_03]: So that then, the mind will learn how to come down within that.

515
00:43:35,316 --> 00:43:44,838
[SPEAKER_03]: Because for as long as there is a possibility for it to go out and relieve itself from, you know, things that are kind of confining, then it will seek it.

516
00:43:45,818 --> 00:43:49,659
[SPEAKER_03]: Only when it renounces, when the senses realize, no, it just, it's not going there.

517
00:43:50,019 --> 00:43:51,279
[SPEAKER_03]: It hasn't been going there for a while.

518
00:43:51,719 --> 00:43:55,880
[SPEAKER_03]: As the Buddha said, the virtue has been developed beforehand.

519
00:43:55,900 --> 00:43:57,120
[SPEAKER_03]: It's a necessary prerequisite.

520
00:43:58,300 --> 00:44:06,028
[SPEAKER_00]: It is counterintuitive at first, because engaging in sensuality does get rid of craving temporarily.

521
00:44:06,269 --> 00:44:10,353
[SPEAKER_03]: Like the dog is screaming and barking and restless and ripping your apartment up.

522
00:44:11,808 --> 00:44:13,209
[SPEAKER_03]: Because it wants food.

523
00:44:13,550 --> 00:44:14,951
[SPEAKER_03]: So, you give it food and it will stop.

524
00:44:16,092 --> 00:44:19,015
[SPEAKER_03]: But see, now, next time we'll do that again, when it wants food.

525
00:44:19,175 --> 00:44:21,177
[SPEAKER_03]: It will cause you damage if you don't give it food.

526
00:44:21,377 --> 00:44:31,667
[SPEAKER_03]: No, actually, yes, I could appease it and get a temporary relief, but as a result of it, I am perpetuating that habit, that it's undermining me further and making things worse and worse.

527
00:44:32,807 --> 00:44:34,088
[SPEAKER_03]: That's exactly your senses.

528
00:44:34,109 --> 00:44:34,569
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

529
00:44:34,589 --> 00:44:36,050
[SPEAKER_04]: That's the five senses.

530
00:44:37,051 --> 00:44:42,895
[SPEAKER_04]: And it's really only by, you know, if you first stop, it's only then that you... You realize how wild the animal is.

531
00:44:42,915 --> 00:44:43,076
[SPEAKER_04]: Yes.

532
00:44:43,096 --> 00:44:49,781
[SPEAKER_03]: Understand that that's the only reason I delight is because the... You will not even recognize the strength of the pull until you stop.

533
00:44:50,081 --> 00:44:50,281
[SPEAKER_03]: Why?

534
00:44:50,321 --> 00:44:51,422
[SPEAKER_03]: Because you've been running with it.

535
00:44:52,023 --> 00:44:55,525
[SPEAKER_03]: But when you stop, then you realize the weight, the intensity of it.

536
00:44:55,565 --> 00:44:56,326
[SPEAKER_00]: Against the grain.

537
00:44:56,506 --> 00:44:56,846
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

538
00:44:56,967 --> 00:44:57,447
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

539
00:44:57,747 --> 00:45:12,332
[SPEAKER_04]: And that's also, like, the way, I think, to understand the difference between, like, the principle of sensuality, or how I would put it, and, let's say, it's not about just, like, specific types of objects, for example.

540
00:45:12,372 --> 00:45:12,492
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah.

541
00:45:12,512 --> 00:45:14,373
[SPEAKER_04]: It's really the principle of that being pulled.

542
00:45:14,413 --> 00:45:14,653
[SPEAKER_00]: Sure.

543
00:45:15,333 --> 00:45:18,034
[SPEAKER_04]: And I think it's important to maybe emphasize that.

544
00:45:18,634 --> 00:45:24,757
[SPEAKER_03]: In the broadest sense, you start with a specific object, specific acts, as I said, that always imply delight and so on.

545
00:45:24,817 --> 00:45:34,041
[SPEAKER_03]: But, yeah, that's just to contain yourself, so that you realize that delight is what colors the picture, wholesome or unwholesome.

546
00:45:35,361 --> 00:45:42,905
[SPEAKER_03]: So then, I guess, the next point would be danger, to kind of cover the gratification.

547
00:45:44,205 --> 00:45:48,067
[SPEAKER_03]: What is the danger aspect, practically, acutely?

548
00:45:49,450 --> 00:45:51,592
[SPEAKER_03]: What is the danger in delight?

549
00:45:53,053 --> 00:45:55,535
[SPEAKER_03]: So, presence of delight means danger.

550
00:45:56,696 --> 00:45:56,896
[SPEAKER_03]: Why?

551
00:45:59,498 --> 00:45:59,838
[SPEAKER_00]: Nature.

552
00:46:00,439 --> 00:46:01,099
[SPEAKER_00]: We're impermanent.

553
00:46:02,120 --> 00:46:04,102
[SPEAKER_00]: You're inserting yourself.

554
00:46:05,523 --> 00:46:08,686
[SPEAKER_03]: But, you know, things remain impermanent either way.

555
00:46:09,286 --> 00:46:12,128
[SPEAKER_03]: Why is it dangerous for me, that impermanence?

556
00:46:12,829 --> 00:46:17,413
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you're perpetuating this assumption of

557
00:46:18,518 --> 00:46:30,127
[SPEAKER_00]: Being able to control your feelings, which is a false assumption, and so, you're making yourself liable to the impermanence of feelings.

558
00:46:30,147 --> 00:46:30,367
[SPEAKER_03]: How?

559
00:46:30,507 --> 00:46:31,027
[SPEAKER_03]: That's the point.

560
00:46:31,067 --> 00:46:32,729
[SPEAKER_03]: How am I making myself liable?

561
00:46:33,149 --> 00:46:43,717
[SPEAKER_03]: Where is that liability that I'm responsible for, so that this delight is dangerous, making me susceptible to danger?

562
00:46:45,755 --> 00:46:47,419
[SPEAKER_03]: In that assumption... Of?

563
00:46:47,841 --> 00:46:48,161
[SPEAKER_03]: Which one?

564
00:46:48,863 --> 00:46:49,445
[SPEAKER_03]: Of sensuality.

565
00:46:50,487 --> 00:46:52,613
[SPEAKER_03]: But no, the assumption of sensuality is because of the delight.

566
00:46:53,971 --> 00:47:08,183
[SPEAKER_04]: Because when you delight, as in you follow after one of these wild animals, you kind of make yourself part, you become the sense, in a way, you assume the sense.

567
00:47:08,203 --> 00:47:11,285
[SPEAKER_03]: Yes, you take on the ownership whether you want it or not.

568
00:47:11,385 --> 00:47:15,409
[SPEAKER_04]: So, you are taking on that ownership, therefore, it's you who are in some place.

569
00:47:15,429 --> 00:47:20,613
[SPEAKER_03]: So, would you be taking the ownership even if you don't follow that animal?

570
00:47:21,683 --> 00:47:23,284
[SPEAKER_03]: But the delight is there.

571
00:47:23,524 --> 00:47:26,467
[SPEAKER_03]: You're already delighted, but you're not following.

572
00:47:26,667 --> 00:47:27,448
[SPEAKER_03]: You are restrained.

573
00:47:28,829 --> 00:47:30,570
[SPEAKER_03]: Would that be implying the ownership?

574
00:47:33,232 --> 00:47:37,235
[SPEAKER_03]: So, you don't go all the way of following, becoming dependent on pleasure.

575
00:47:38,176 --> 00:47:39,217
[SPEAKER_03]: But the delight is there.

576
00:47:39,437 --> 00:47:41,819
[SPEAKER_03]: So, if the delight is there, is the ownership there?

577
00:47:45,142 --> 00:47:47,003
[SPEAKER_03]: Or it takes time for the ownership to develop?

578
00:47:49,042 --> 00:47:50,423
[SPEAKER_04]: It takes time for the ownership to deliver.

579
00:47:50,463 --> 00:47:51,705
[SPEAKER_04]: No, no.

580
00:47:51,725 --> 00:47:52,806
[SPEAKER_02]: It's not like...

581
00:47:52,846 --> 00:48:01,393
[SPEAKER_02]: This is... you can see in terms of the five holding aggregates, which is what sakāyā is.

582
00:48:01,634 --> 00:48:04,196
[SPEAKER_02]: And, you know, is that holding separate or not?

583
00:48:04,256 --> 00:48:05,617
[SPEAKER_02]: No, it's the delight and lust.

584
00:48:06,158 --> 00:48:07,659
[SPEAKER_02]: Exactly.

585
00:48:07,719 --> 00:48:10,001
[SPEAKER_02]: I would say for the six senses, it's basically the same.

586
00:48:10,361 --> 00:48:10,582
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

587
00:48:11,222 --> 00:48:19,682
[SPEAKER_03]: So, presence of delight is presence of danger, because presence of delight is presence of ownership of that which you cannot own.

588
00:48:21,831 --> 00:48:36,941
[SPEAKER_02]: So, is it then valid for someone who has been following our discussion so far, to start making, at least in their thinking, a clear distinction between, let's say, me here, and six senses, which are wild animals doing their own thing, and I want to keep as separate from that as possible.

589
00:48:37,722 --> 00:48:42,025
[SPEAKER_02]: Not projecting myself into those senses.

590
00:48:43,326 --> 00:48:44,126
[SPEAKER_03]: Ah, no.

591
00:48:44,607 --> 00:48:46,168
[SPEAKER_03]: No, it's not on the level of projection.

592
00:48:47,256 --> 00:48:58,425
[SPEAKER_03]: Basically, first, what person would need to do is recognize that if they think about the senses separate from them, the six animals, or them separate from the senses, that's already the result of the six animals.

593
00:48:59,325 --> 00:49:11,275
[SPEAKER_03]: So, first, they need to find the practical examples of the six animals pulling them, their delighting in the aspect of relief, and then discern them, themselves, on that level.

594
00:49:13,365 --> 00:49:22,630
[SPEAKER_03]: In other words, if you want to contemplate yourself, separate from the animals, you do that on the basis of the animal pull, pressure, pain you have to endure.

595
00:49:23,351 --> 00:49:27,833
[SPEAKER_02]: Yes, so you're going to see yourself in that sense, whether the light, with those senses.

596
00:49:28,552 --> 00:49:29,673
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, well, that's what I'm saying.

597
00:49:29,853 --> 00:49:31,374
[SPEAKER_03]: That's where your sense of self is.

598
00:49:31,414 --> 00:49:31,734
[SPEAKER_03]: Exactly.

599
00:49:32,114 --> 00:49:36,657
[SPEAKER_03]: Like, you might not see yourself there, if you expect to see yourself elsewhere or in different terms.

600
00:49:36,817 --> 00:49:44,743
[SPEAKER_03]: So, you've got to abandon your expectations of where myself is and start enduring things on the right level and then, again, make the effort

601
00:49:45,363 --> 00:49:51,687
[SPEAKER_03]: To basically prevent yourself from interpreting yourself in these external terms, and actually see that...

602
00:49:51,727 --> 00:49:56,951
[SPEAKER_03]: I might not see it now, I might see it later, but if I understand this correctly, my sense of ownership, i.e.

603
00:49:57,031 --> 00:50:03,695
[SPEAKER_03]: my sense of self, is here on this level of basically pressure of the senses that I don't want to endure, that I'd like to get rid of.

604
00:50:04,817 --> 00:50:06,258
[SPEAKER_03]: That's what it is.

605
00:50:06,638 --> 00:50:18,123
[SPEAKER_03]: So, I don't think you could just extrapolate the contemplation of the self separate from the senses and do it independent of the practical basis of the endurance of the five animals.

606
00:50:18,143 --> 00:50:18,303
[SPEAKER_03]: No.

607
00:50:18,503 --> 00:50:19,303
[SPEAKER_03]: That's kind of the point.

608
00:50:19,443 --> 00:50:19,664
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

609
00:50:19,724 --> 00:50:19,944
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

610
00:50:19,964 --> 00:50:24,205
[SPEAKER_02]: Because the starting point will be that there's already identification with the senses.

611
00:50:24,225 --> 00:50:24,746
[SPEAKER_02]: Exactly.

612
00:50:24,766 --> 00:50:24,926
[SPEAKER_02]: Exactly.

613
00:50:24,946 --> 00:50:27,947
[SPEAKER_02]: You cannot just decide to step out of the whole domain of senses.

614
00:50:28,327 --> 00:50:28,767
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

615
00:50:28,787 --> 00:50:30,908
[SPEAKER_02]: So, the closest you can come to discerning

616
00:50:36,833 --> 00:50:38,735
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

617
00:50:38,875 --> 00:50:56,693
[SPEAKER_03]: So then, practically, the closest you could come to discerning the not-self is to enduring the pull of the animals, without covering it up, without giving in, without justifying it, trying to get rid of it, without managing it, psychologizing it.

618
00:50:57,545 --> 00:51:04,691
[SPEAKER_03]: Because that is that which will truly reveal to you how much you don't own your own senses, your own self.

619
00:51:05,312 --> 00:51:09,635
[SPEAKER_03]: How much, how wild they are, and you, like, you don't even exist for them.

620
00:51:10,196 --> 00:51:13,499
[SPEAKER_03]: A wild dog, you are just a feature in the world.

621
00:51:13,619 --> 00:51:16,861
[SPEAKER_03]: It doesn't care for you, it doesn't respect you, it doesn't listen to you, that's for sure.

622
00:51:17,642 --> 00:51:33,365
[SPEAKER_03]: So, but people do, see, by constantly acting out and running out with these animals, so they don't feel the tension, they develop this, oh, yeah, this is I am, this is mine, uh, my senses obey me, because, look, I can send them here, I can send them there, I can pull them to run to the left.

623
00:51:33,385 --> 00:51:35,906
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, but you can't stop, because if you stop, they will rip you apart.

624
00:51:37,286 --> 00:51:40,487
[SPEAKER_03]: Oh, so you're very much just their servant, a convenient servant.

625
00:51:40,627 --> 00:51:41,407
[SPEAKER_03]: Yes, they need you,

626
00:51:42,658 --> 00:51:55,343
[SPEAKER_03]: For directing, like five, five organs, five sense organs need the mind, they meet in it, they get the sense from it, they get the meaning from it, the whole possibilities, but in reality, it's like, which one comes first?

627
00:51:55,443 --> 00:51:57,623
[SPEAKER_03]: Which one is subjugated to the other one?

628
00:51:58,104 --> 00:51:59,104
[SPEAKER_03]: Which one is the servant?

629
00:52:00,064 --> 00:52:06,967
[SPEAKER_03]: Is the mind the servant of the five senses, or has the mind developed so the five senses are basically subordinate to the mind?

630
00:52:07,912 --> 00:52:11,715
[SPEAKER_03]: And yes, for most people, the mind is the servant of the senses, through and through.

631
00:52:12,916 --> 00:52:19,502
[SPEAKER_03]: Although the mind maintains this arrogant assumption that, you know, this is mine, I am in charge, because look, I can turn left, I can turn right.

632
00:52:20,563 --> 00:52:21,344
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, but can you stop?

633
00:52:21,544 --> 00:52:32,313
[SPEAKER_01]: But once the senses have calmed down, you've endured the pressure long enough, they've calmed down, then what is revealed is who now is the master.

634
00:52:32,433 --> 00:52:32,653
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

635
00:52:33,654 --> 00:52:35,456
[SPEAKER_03]: The unknown ability, you realize, oh, these

636
00:52:36,282 --> 00:52:40,049
[SPEAKER_03]: I cannot be these animals, because if I were, they would be in my control.

637
00:52:40,169 --> 00:52:42,753
[SPEAKER_03]: But yet, look at them, kicking and screaming.

638
00:52:42,773 --> 00:52:43,835
[SPEAKER_03]: So, I can only tame them.

639
00:52:45,217 --> 00:52:46,579
[SPEAKER_03]: I can only calm them down.

640
00:52:47,201 --> 00:52:48,262
[SPEAKER_03]: But I cannot be them.

641
00:52:48,843 --> 00:52:49,745
[SPEAKER_03]: They cannot be mine.

642
00:52:51,153 --> 00:52:57,675
[SPEAKER_02]: Yes, and so, one can discern the senses already being present, already hunting and chasing things.

643
00:52:58,495 --> 00:53:00,516
[SPEAKER_02]: And secondly, it's not just one sense doing that.

644
00:53:00,816 --> 00:53:04,477
[SPEAKER_02]: It's actually five, simultaneously, in different directions.

645
00:53:04,497 --> 00:53:05,617
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, in their own domains, yeah.

646
00:53:05,637 --> 00:53:07,618
[SPEAKER_02]: So, then, eh...

647
00:53:08,818 --> 00:53:17,840
[SPEAKER_02]: That already undercuts the idea of there being one owner, because it's like walking with five or six different animals on a leash, going in all directions.

648
00:53:17,860 --> 00:53:19,940
[SPEAKER_02]: So, where do you think you're going with all that?

649
00:53:20,240 --> 00:53:27,882
[SPEAKER_03]: And the problem for people is, because if you've been running with them, so you don't experience the pain and the pressure and the pull, you stop seeing them.

650
00:53:28,522 --> 00:53:29,742
[SPEAKER_03]: All you see is yourself running.

651
00:53:30,843 --> 00:53:33,183
[SPEAKER_03]: So, people don't even see the five senses for where they are.

652
00:53:34,063 --> 00:53:37,084
[SPEAKER_03]: And that's exactly that identifying aspect.

653
00:53:37,759 --> 00:53:40,000
[SPEAKER_03]: That gets developed on account of that assumed ownership.

654
00:53:40,120 --> 00:53:41,441
[SPEAKER_03]: Like, you think you are it.

655
00:53:41,761 --> 00:53:43,942
[SPEAKER_03]: You think you are what you see here, smelted.

656
00:53:44,202 --> 00:53:45,462
[SPEAKER_03]: You think you are those senses.

657
00:53:46,683 --> 00:53:50,885
[SPEAKER_03]: So, you put those views, those ideas, first, as we started in the beginning, as we said that.

658
00:53:51,785 --> 00:53:58,428
[SPEAKER_03]: Failing to realize, no, that there are actually physical organs of eyes, nose, the five senses.

659
00:53:59,511 --> 00:54:06,355
[SPEAKER_03]: That I cannot be identified with, even if I want to, because they're endured there in the world, subjected to elements and everything else.

660
00:54:06,415 --> 00:54:10,037
[SPEAKER_03]: So, that contemplation can be enough to make... get you started, so to speak.

661
00:54:10,077 --> 00:54:12,418
[SPEAKER_03]: So, you... you don't identify as much.

662
00:54:12,859 --> 00:54:21,123
[SPEAKER_03]: But, fundamentally, it's gonna boil down whether you're still acting towards it or not, whether you're able to endure the delight for as far as it goes, as sharp as it gets.

663
00:54:21,684 --> 00:54:25,406
[SPEAKER_03]: That... but only if I touch this, the relief will happen.

664
00:54:25,446 --> 00:54:27,027
[SPEAKER_03]: Just... just... just this tiny... no.

665
00:54:28,485 --> 00:54:31,166
[SPEAKER_03]: Because it would be done for the wrong reason out of that perversion.

666
00:54:31,847 --> 00:54:33,668
[SPEAKER_03]: If that's... if the delight is not there, that's fine.

667
00:54:33,748 --> 00:54:34,808
[SPEAKER_03]: Then things are not an issue.

668
00:54:35,489 --> 00:54:38,170
[SPEAKER_03]: If the light is not present, if you have uprooted it.

669
00:54:38,770 --> 00:54:45,494
[SPEAKER_03]: But until that happens, you have to recognize that the danger is not in everything else that would follow on a kind of a gauge.

670
00:54:45,534 --> 00:54:46,114
[SPEAKER_03]: Yes, it is.

671
00:54:46,714 --> 00:54:51,457
[SPEAKER_03]: But the more acute danger of sensuality is on the level of presence of delight.

672
00:54:52,522 --> 00:54:54,863
[SPEAKER_03]: Means ownership is already there.

673
00:54:55,304 --> 00:54:59,206
[SPEAKER_03]: Means wild animals are in charge, at least to that extent.

674
00:54:59,907 --> 00:55:09,873
[SPEAKER_03]: And if you were to recognize truly that even like a second of that perversion of the order implies the danger of the entire domain, you lose everything else possible.

675
00:55:10,073 --> 00:55:11,914
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, you realize, oh no, this is...

676
00:55:12,074 --> 00:55:12,694
[SPEAKER_03]: It doesn't matter.

677
00:55:12,734 --> 00:55:14,135
[SPEAKER_03]: Oh, well, it's not as much as before.

678
00:55:14,155 --> 00:55:14,715
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, that's good.

679
00:55:14,776 --> 00:55:17,017
[SPEAKER_03]: But just the grain of it is equally bad.

680
00:55:19,235 --> 00:55:19,996
[SPEAKER_03]: A grain of delight.

681
00:55:20,276 --> 00:55:28,182
[SPEAKER_02]: And this is why a non-disciple... a noble disciple can make progress in dhamma by developing the perception of non-delight in the entire world.

682
00:55:28,283 --> 00:55:29,103
[SPEAKER_02]: In the entire world.

683
00:55:29,263 --> 00:55:30,204
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, yeah, yeah.

684
00:55:31,705 --> 00:55:33,287
[SPEAKER_03]: And the Buddha said similar in regard to...

685
00:55:34,645 --> 00:55:35,446
[SPEAKER_03]: Being, isn't it?

686
00:55:36,006 --> 00:55:38,048
[SPEAKER_03]: Like, even the small traces of it.

687
00:55:38,528 --> 00:55:40,109
[SPEAKER_03]: It's like the excrement.

688
00:55:40,230 --> 00:55:43,712
[SPEAKER_03]: Whether it's a, you know, a bucket of it or a tiny... it smells the same.

689
00:55:43,812 --> 00:55:44,273
[SPEAKER_03]: It's horrible.

690
00:55:45,093 --> 00:55:46,134
[SPEAKER_03]: And so, it's a being.

691
00:55:47,175 --> 00:55:47,635
[SPEAKER_03]: Any being.

692
00:55:47,696 --> 00:55:53,340
[SPEAKER_03]: So, whether it's karmabhava or just like being of sensuality or just being cleared from sensuality.

693
00:55:53,360 --> 00:55:57,824
[SPEAKER_03]: Either way, tiny bit of being, it's as bad as a lot of being.

694
00:55:58,764 --> 00:56:01,707
[SPEAKER_03]: It has the same smell, the same danger, the same peril.

695
00:56:05,037 --> 00:56:15,880
[SPEAKER_04]: Like you said, that I find that important, too, not to forget that the senses are these physical things in the world that are just made of matter, subjected to elements.

696
00:56:16,040 --> 00:56:19,521
[SPEAKER_03]: And that's exactly, sorry, like, because we mentioned peripheral in the beginning.

697
00:56:19,641 --> 00:56:22,942
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, see, you find very important to not forget that.

698
00:56:23,402 --> 00:56:26,683
[SPEAKER_03]: And you realize, okay, so it's a type of reminding yourself

699
00:56:27,463 --> 00:56:29,305
[SPEAKER_03]: Through certain contexts and contemplations.

700
00:56:29,705 --> 00:56:32,388
[SPEAKER_03]: And that's how you practice that peripheral awareness.

701
00:56:32,408 --> 00:56:34,990
[SPEAKER_03]: See, because you cannot directly perceive those senses.

702
00:56:35,611 --> 00:56:43,378
[SPEAKER_03]: But by reminding of the right order, as in they do have to be in the world they're independent or not identified with.

703
00:56:43,759 --> 00:56:47,763
[SPEAKER_03]: Like, in order I can identify with the senses and forget about them, is because they're already there.

704
00:56:48,753 --> 00:56:53,937
[SPEAKER_03]: So, if you never forget that, that's how you practice the peripheral awareness that we spoke about in our previous videos.

705
00:56:54,377 --> 00:56:57,379
[SPEAKER_03]: Not by some method of, you know, looking peripherally.

706
00:56:57,480 --> 00:56:57,620
[SPEAKER_03]: No.

707
00:56:57,820 --> 00:57:03,724
[SPEAKER_03]: Reminding yourself of the context that's based on the right order and that is why it's helpful.

708
00:57:03,944 --> 00:57:07,107
[SPEAKER_03]: Okay, so just keep that and you can never spill out of it.

709
00:57:07,447 --> 00:57:09,629
[SPEAKER_03]: You know, you can forget, you can get distracted within it.

710
00:57:10,481 --> 00:57:15,863
[SPEAKER_03]: Until you work on that aspect of things, but fundamentally, that will be your kind of outer wall.

711
00:57:16,643 --> 00:57:18,244
[SPEAKER_03]: These senses, I cannot go outside of.

712
00:57:18,384 --> 00:57:19,624
[SPEAKER_03]: I cannot spill outside.

713
00:57:19,904 --> 00:57:20,764
[SPEAKER_03]: I am within them.

714
00:57:20,864 --> 00:57:21,825
[SPEAKER_03]: I am contained in them.

715
00:57:22,385 --> 00:57:25,026
[SPEAKER_03]: They are my ways of accessing the world.

716
00:57:26,606 --> 00:57:28,847
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah, and so, therefore, that even makes the...

717
00:57:29,955 --> 00:57:38,057
[SPEAKER_04]: The danger of ownership of them, even worse in a way, because not only are you this constant slave of them, but...

718
00:57:38,077 --> 00:57:39,737
[SPEAKER_03]: They're bound for destruction.

719
00:57:39,757 --> 00:57:42,037
[SPEAKER_03]: And you're going that way if you don't abandon them.

720
00:57:42,237 --> 00:57:42,417
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

721
00:57:43,057 --> 00:57:44,338
[SPEAKER_04]: And that becomes your destruction.

722
00:57:44,478 --> 00:57:45,018
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, exactly.

723
00:57:45,038 --> 00:57:45,698
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, yeah.

724
00:57:45,978 --> 00:57:47,678
